  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nThe passing of a cold front this evening will bring a strong \npressure gradient over the area. The front will usher in dry and\ncolder air, allowing for mixing to occur overnight and gusty \nwinds can be expected through this evening. Winds could gust \nbetween 25 and 35 mph, and could lead to some unsecured objects,\nlike tents, and patio furniture blowing away. These northwest \nwinds will also make today feel chilly, as high temperatures \ncould struggle to even reach 50F away from the immediate coast.\n\nFor hikers: Conditions will be dangerous and wintry in the \nhigher terrain. In addition to snow showers, winds will be much \nhigher and and temperatures much colder, making for winds chills\nin the single digits to possibly below zero above tree line. \n\nHigh pressure looks to build overhead tonight, which will allow\nfor the winds to calm. Calm winds and clear skies should create\nradiational cooling conditions. Low temperatures in the 20s are\nforecast, but some places north of the mountains could see lows\nin the teens for the first time since last winter.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nStarting on Sunday, an upper-level trough exits the \nnortheastern US and is replaced by a broad ridge. In general, \nupper-level ridging should allow for temperatures to be more \nmild through much of next week and temperatures do look to run a\nlittle above average on Sunday and Monday. However, high \npressure to the north especially towards the middle of next week\nmay lead to more seasonable temperatures then. No precipitation\nis forecast next week.",
